save(i,dict(title='Necessary logarithmic amplification when composing XOR with a total function',formal='Historical existence question, resolved affirmatively: are there total nonconstant Boolean functions g for which the randomized query cost of XOR of n independent input blocks is Ω(n log n · R(g))? In explicit asymptotic terms, seek an absolute c>0 and, for arbitrarily large n, an input length m=m(n) and total nonconstant gₙ:{0,1}ᵐ→{0,1} with R(XORₙ∘gₙⁿ)≥c n log₂ n R(gₙ). The source also permits the analogous majority lower bound as an alternative affirmative answer. Corollary 1.3 of the 2023 paper establishes the XOR alternative. The inner function and its input length may grow with n; the claim is not about a single fixed finite truth table for unbounded n.',definitions='For input blocks x¹,…,xⁿ∈{0,1}ᵐ, (XORₙ∘gⁿ)(x¹,…,xⁿ)=g(x¹)⊕⋯⊕g(xⁿ), where ⊕ is addition modulo two. The blocks are separate parts of an arbitrary worst-case input; they are not promised to be randomly sampled. A randomized decision tree chooses which input bit to read next from previously read bits and internal random coins. Each bit read costs one query. R(h) is the smallest q for which such an algorithm always makes at most q queries, for every input and every coin outcome, and outputs h correctly with probability at least 2/3 on every input. There is no abort outcome and no postselection. Internal computation and the description of h are free; this is decision-tree complexity, denoted BPP(h) in the source, not the uniform time class BPP. Total means g is defined on all 2ᵐ inputs. The comparison uses the optimal cost of one copy, not the cost of a particular algorithm for g. In the source’s alternative, MAJₙ outputs one when at least n/2 of its input bits are one, including a tie. The later theorem also uses R̄δ(h): the minimum, over algorithms with error at most δ on every input, of the maximum over inputs of their expected query count. R̄ and R are distinct cost conventions; the stated resolved target uses R on both sides.',question_type='yes_no',answer_criterion='An unbounded family satisfying the displayed worst-case, 1/3-error lower bound for XOR answers the source question affirmatively; the generic O(n log n R(g)) upper bound makes the dependence tight up to constants. A majority family with the same lower bound would also answer the original disjunction, but is unnecessary once XOR is established. A promise function, noisy input oracle or postselection lower bound alone would not meet the total-function target.',context_blocks=[dict(title='The extra factor is the subject of the question',text='The source compares the cost of evaluating a composition with n times the cost of one inner function. It asks whether the additional logarithmic overhead can be necessary when the inner function has no promise on its inputs.',citation='primary'),dict(title='A growing family is essential',text='For fixed m, reading all nm bits bounds composition cost by nm. The intended asymptotic question therefore permits m and g to vary as the number of blocks grows. The total functions used in the cited error-reduction result have an explicit admissible error range tied to their growing input length.',citation='inner'),dict(title='The later theorem answers the exact total-function question',text='Brody, Kim, Lerdputtipongporn and Srinivasulu state the total-function consequence as Corollary 1.3 and explicitly identify it as answering Ben-David and colleagues’ question. Although their general direct-sum theorem uses expected query complexity, this corollary gives the requested worst-case randomized query bound.',citation='solution'),dict(title='The majority alternative remains a separate assertion',text='The extracted card stopped before the word or and its majority alternative. Recovering the full sentence matters for the answer criterion: the XOR result suffices to resolve this existence question without a claim that both alternatives have been proved.',citation='primary')],why='Shows that error reduction can impose a genuine logarithmic cost in composing randomized computations even when every inner input is valid.',references=refs,progress=[dict(date='2019',text='A total-function error-reduction separation is proved in the expected-query model.',citation='inner'),dict(date='2020',text='The source asks whether logarithmic amplification is necessary for total inner functions under XOR or majority; a July follow-up answers the XOR case.',citation='solution'),dict(date='2023-12-31',text='The direct-sum theorem and total-function corollary are published in Theory of Computing.',citation='solution')],status='resolved',status_note='Matched Open Question 12, including its truncated majority alternative, to the explicit total-function Corollary 1.3. Read the worst-case/expected definitions and the supporting 2019 theorem’s parameter regime. Verified the final 2023 publication and searched subsequent XOR literature. No independent proof audit is claimed, and no majority theorem is inferred.',model_self_contained=True,requires_context=False,importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='An exact composition-overhead phenomenon for total randomized decision-tree problems. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),review_note='Recovered the full disjunction, clarified growing inner functions and worst-case query cost, and marked resolved by the published XOR corollary.',working_summary=dict(sentences=['The task is to output the parity of n values of the same total Boolean function on separate input blocks.','The source asks whether this can require n log n times the cost of computing one value.','Cost is worst-case randomized bit queries with error at most one third on every input.','A published 2023 corollary gives a growing family of total functions attaining the logarithmic overhead.','This resolves the original XOR-or-majority existence question through its XOR alternative.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','inner','solution'])),notes=['Recovered XOR-or-majority disjunction and no-promise inner-function condition.','Defined worst-case error and query depth, block composition and growing-family quantifiers.','Separated expected-query direct-sum theorem from its worst-case total-function corollary.','Matched direct published resolution and marked resolved.'],checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs]+['https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.05580','https://theoryofcomputing.org/articles/v019a011/'])
